WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.060
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.680
AI research has come a long-long way in the
last few years.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.490
I remember that not so long ago, we were lucky
if we could train a neural network to understand

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:19.090
traffic signs, and since then, so many things
happened: by harnessing the power of learning

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:25.080
algorithms, we are now able to impersonate
other people by using a consumer camera, generate

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.349
high-quality virtual human faces for people
that don’t exist, or pretend to be able

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:38.210
to dance as a pro dancer by using an external
video footage and transferring it onto ourselves.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.840
Even though we are progressing at a staggering
pace, there is a lot of debate as to which

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.590
research direction is the most promising going
forward.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.380
Roughly speaking, there are two schools of
thought.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.180
One, we recently talked about Richard Sutton’s
amazing article by the name, “The Bitter

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.850
Lesson”, in which he makes a great argument
that AI research should not try to mimic the

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:06.520
way the human brain works - he argues that
instead, all we need to do is formulate our

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.840
problems in a general manner, so that our
learning algorithms may find something that

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.020
is potentially much better suited for a problem
than our brain is.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.800
I put a link to this video in the description
if you’re interested.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.480
And two, a different school of thought says
that we should a good look at all these learning

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.330
algorithms that use a lot of powerful hardware
and can do wondrous things, like playing a

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.290
bunch of Atari games at a superhuman level.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:38.460
Note that they learn orders of magnitude slower
than the human brain does, so it should definitely

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.530
be worth it to try to study and model the
human brain, at least until we can match it

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:45.860
in terms of efficiency.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.040
This school of thought is what we are going
to talk about in this video.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.120
As an example, let’s take a look at deep
reinforcement learning in the context of playing

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.550
computer games.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.360
This technique is a combination of a neural
network that processes the visual data that

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.040
we see on the screen, and a reinforcement
learner that comes up with the gameplay-related

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.090
decisions.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:12.200
Absolutely amazing algorithm, a true breakthrough
in AI research.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.150
Very powerful, however, also quite slow.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:21.489
And by slow, I mean that we can sit for an
hour in front of our computer and wonder why

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:27.219
our learner does not work at all, because
it loses all of its lives almost immediately.

00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:32.620
If we remain patient, we find out that it
works, it just learns at a glacial pace.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.129
So, why is this so slow?

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:37.430
Well, two reasons.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.650
Reason number one is that the learning happens
through incremental parameter adjustment.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:42.730
What does that mean?

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.829
If a human fails really badly at a task, the
human would know that a drastic adjustment

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:52.629
to the strategy is necessary, while the deep
reinforcement learner would start applying

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:58.099
tiny, tiny changes to its behavior and test
again if things got better.

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:02.760
This takes a while, and as a result, this
seems unlikely to have a close relation to

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.010
how we, humans think.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:10.079
The second reason for it being slow is the
presence of weak inductive bias.

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:14.159
This means that the learner does not contain
any information about the problem we have

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:19.969
at hand, or in other words, has never seen
the game we’re playing before and has no

00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:23.269
other previous knowledge about games at all.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:28.620
This is desirable in some cases, because we
can reuse one learning algorithm for a variety

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.889
of problems.

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:36.209
However, because this way, the AI has to test
a stupendously large number of potential hypotheses

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:42.380
about the game, we will have to pay for this
convenience by a mighty inefficient algorithm.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.319
But is this really all true?

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:49.050
Does deep reinforcement learning really have
to be so slow?

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.049
And what on earth does this have to do with
our brain?

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:58.980
Well, this paper proposes an interesting counterargument
that this is not necessarily true and argues

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.159
that with two well thought out changes, the
efficiency of deep reinforcement learning

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:09.249
may be drastically improved, and get this,
it also tells us that these changes are also

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.890
possibly based in neuroscience.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.689
So what are the two changes?

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:18.740
One is using episodic memory, which stores
previous experiences to help estimating the

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.490
potential value of different actions, and
this way, drastic parameter adjustments become

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.820
a possibility.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.321
And it not only improves the efficiency, but
there is more to it, because there are recent

00:04:30.321 --> 00:04:35.960
studies that show that using episodic memory
indeed contributes to the learning of real

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.620
humans and animals alike.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:44.330
And two, it is beneficial to let the AI implement
its own reinforcement learning algorithm,

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:50.020
a concept often referred to as “learning
to learn” or meta reinforcement learning.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:55.660
This also helps obtaining more general knowledge
that can be reused across tasks, further improving

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.900
the efficiency of the agent.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.360
Here you see a picture of an fMRI, and some
regions are marked with yellow and orange

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.400
here.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.350
What could these possibly mean?

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:11.780
Well, hold on to your papers, because these
highlight neural structures that implement

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.470
a very similar meta reinforcement learning
scheme within the human brain.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:20.760
It turns out that meta reinforcement learning,
or this “learning to learn” scheme may

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.370
not just be something that speeds up our AI
algorithms, but may be a fundamental principle

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.360
of the human brain as well.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.790
So these two changes to deep reinforcement
learning not only drastically improve its

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:37.970
efficiency, but it also suddenly maps quite
a bit better to our brain.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.560
How cool is that?

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.150
So, which school of thought are you most fond
of?

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:48.040
Should we model the brain, or should we listen
to Richard Sutton’s Bitter Lesson?

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.430
Let me know in the comments.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:54.530
Also, make sure to have a look at the paper,
I found it to be quite readable, and you really

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:59.110
don’t need to be a neuroscientist to enjoy
it and learn quite a few new things.

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.360
Make sure to have a look at it in the video
description!

